I'll solve this directly in the TUI using coordinate geometry.

**Proof.**

Place $O$ at the origin. Since $A,O,C$ are collinear and $B,O,D$ are collinear, write
$$A=(a,0),\quad C=(-c,0),\quad B=(b\cos\theta,b\sin\theta),\quad D=(-d\cos\theta,-d\sin\theta)$$
with $a,b,c,d>0$.

A circle through $O$ has equation $x^2+y^2+ux+vy=0$.

**Circle $(OAD)$:** $u=-a$, and from $D$: $v=(d+a\cos\theta)/\sin\theta$. So
$$\omega_1:\ x^2+y^2-ax+\tfrac{d+a\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}y=0.$$

**Circle $(OBC)$:** $u=c$, and from $B$: $v=-(b+c\cos\theta)/\sin\theta$. So
$$\omega_2:\ x^2+y^2+cx-\tfrac{b+c\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}y=0.$$

The radical axis (line $OM$) is $\omega_1-\omega_2=0$:
$$-(a+c)x+\tfrac{d+b+(a+c)\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}\,y=0.$$
Set $P=d+b+(a+c)\cos\theta$ and $Q=(a+c)\sin\theta$; parametrize line $OM$ by
$$(x,y)=t\,(P,Q).$$

**Finding $M$** (second intersection of $\omega_1,\omega_2$): substituting into $\omega_1$,
$$t^2(P^2+Q^2)+t\!\left[-aP+(d+a\cos\theta)(a+c)\right]=0,$$
using $Q/\sin\theta=a+c$. The nonzero root is
$$t_M=\frac{aP-(d+a\cos\theta)(a+c)}{P^2+Q^2}=\frac{ab-cd}{P^2+Q^2},$$
since $aP-(d+a\cos\theta)(a+c)=a(d+b)-d(a+c)=ab-cd$.

**Finding $T$** (second intersection of $OM$ with circle $(OAB)$): circle $(OAB)$ has $u=-a$, $v=(a\cos\theta-b)/\sin\theta$. Substituting $(tP,tQ)$:
$$t^2(P^2+Q^2)+t\!\left[-aP+(a\cos\theta-b)(a+c)\right]=0,$$
so
$$t_T=\frac{aP-(a\cos\theta-b)(a+c)}{P^2+Q^2}=\frac{a(d+b)+b(a+c)}{P^2+Q^2}=\frac{ad+2ab+bc}{P^2+Q^2}.$$

**Finding $S$** (second intersection of $OM$ with circle $(OCD)$): circle $(OCD)$ has $u=c$, $v=(d-c\cos\theta)/\sin\theta$. Substituting $(tP,tQ)$:
$$t^2(P^2+Q^2)+t\!\left[cP+(d-c\cos\theta)(a+c)\right]=0,$$
so
$$t_S=-\frac{cP+(d-c\cos\theta)(a+c)}{P^2+Q^2}=-\frac{c(d+b)+d(a+c)}{P^2+Q^2}=-\frac{ad+bc+2cd}{P^2+Q^2}.$$

**Midpoint check:**
$$\frac{t_S+t_T}{2}=\frac{-(ad+bc+2cd)+(ad+2ab+bc)}{2(P^2+Q^2)}=\frac{2(ab-cd)}{2(P^2+Q^2)}=\frac{ab-cd}{P^2+Q^2}=t_M.$$

Since $S,M,T$ are collinear on line $OM$ with parameters $t_S,t_M,t_T$ and $t_M=(t_S+t_T)/2$, the point $M$ is the midpoint of $ST$. $\blacksquare$

